    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d260">

                <pb facs="0491" n="205" xml:id="pg205"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2481</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-03-09T10:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 9, 1916, 10 a. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 10, 4.26 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3601. Papers report House took same action as Senate on resolution to warn
                    Americans by vote of 258 to 160. Cologne <hi rendition="#italic">Gazette</hi>
                    complains of negligence of this Embassy in not telegraphing contents of secret
                    orders contained in enclosures to German memorandum to Washington, thus giving
                    British Government chance to get in first with misleading statements. As result
                    the debates in Congress do not seem to have touched the question of aggressive
                    armament of English merchantmen, which is the main point of the German
                    argument.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
